  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on stated preference survey data rather than observed real-world behavior, which may differ as actual autonomous vehicle technology and sharing markets mature.",
      "what": "This study models how private autonomous vehicle owners choose to deploy their idle vehicles among parking, cruising, or sharing under uncertain fees and revenues. Using stated preference survey data from Japan, the researchers estimated a hybrid choice model based on cumulative prospect theory to capture how latent attitudes, such as trust in safety and privacy concerns, influence these decisions.",
      "why": "The model quantifies how non-monetary factors shape AV deployment, showing that trust in safety increases sharing while privacy concerns suppress it, and positive technology attitudes encourage traffic-generating cruising behavior. This provides a framework for planners to anticipate how subjective user attitudes, rather than just pricing, will impact urban traffic congestion and parking demand."
    }
